{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789402506,"nonce":"AHnT1lVLX8dWVuM16AJyo+9oBE4=","message":"First stage landed in ff768c0, d44738c and 85b081c: a conservative, non-moving mark-sweep over the pool's slabs, cooperating with the drop analysis (an explicit free is an immediate reclaim; the collector only sees what the analysis could not prove; blocks already free are excluded from marking; the trigger is heap growth, so a program the analysis covers never collects). Roots: native stacks with callee-saved registers spilled, mutable globals, fiber stacks and environments; heap words scanned conservatively. Strings and boxes from plugins and the runtime come from the pool now so they are collectable too.\n\nMeasured on the pressure programs at 200,000 steps: closures 82 -\u003e 30 MB, dicts 141 -\u003e 31 MB, generators 1.3 GB -\u003e 29 MB, tree 214 -\u003e 30 MB, all flat between 2,000 and 200,000 steps. binary_trees depth 16: 0.42 s at 49 MB RSS against 0.42 s at about 1 GB before (78 collections of about 2.5 ms: mark 1.8 ms for the 4 MB live tree, sweep 0.7 ms). op_overload, nbody, mandelbrot: no collection, no change.\n\nWhat remains of the Immix design: line-based bump allocation over the same 64 KB slabs in place of the size-class free lists (sweep would then flip bitmaps rather than thread every freed block, and allocation would lose its per-request TLS pop, 1142d23), and evacuation once the frontend emits pointer maps for heap objects, with conservatively referenced objects pinned. Generational or incremental marking would need a write barrier in compiled code and is not planned until a workload shows pauses.","files":null}]}